dated from 0 to 1, and the reply appeared immediately under 'Replies (1)' with correct author (TechExplorer) and text.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-5\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Clicked the upvote button on the thread itself: count went from 0 to 1 and button showed active state. Clicked the upvote button on the posted reply: count went from 0 to 1 and button showed active state. Both updates reflected immediately without page reload.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-6\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Typed 'mechanical keyboard' into the header search box on the homepage. After a short debounce, the page heading subtitle changed to 'Showing results for \\\\\\\"mechanical keyboard\\\\\\\"' and the thread list filtered down from 9 threads to exactly 1 matching result: 'Building a mechanical keyboard from scratch', whose title and body contain the keywords.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-7\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Clicked 'Gaming (312)' category link in the sidebar. Navigated to /category/gaming, page heading changed to 'Gaming' with description 'Video games, board games, and esports', and only the thread belonging to the Gaming category ('Best indie games of 2023 that flew under the radar') was displayed, excluding all threads from other categories.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-8\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Homepage 'Trending' view (default) sorted threads by upvote score descending (156,124,92,89,78,67,56,47,1). Clicked 'Most Active' tab: heading changed to 'Most Active Discussions' and thread order re-sorted by reply count descending (89,67,45,41,38,34,31,23,1 replies), confirming activity-based sorting.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-9\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Reply history not shown on profile\", \"actual\": \"Visited own profile (/profile/user-1) after posting a new thread and a reply on that thread. The profile page only shows a 'Threads by TechExplorer (3)' section listing threads started by the user. There is no section, tab, or list showing the user's reply history (the reply posted earlier in this session, 'This is my first reply to test the reply functionality.', does not appear anywhere on the profile page).\", \"evidence\": \"Profile page body text only contains 'Threads by TechExplorer (3)' with 3 thread entries; no 'Replies by TechExplorer' section exists.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-10\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Clicked on 'NewsHound' author link from a thread card on the homepage while logged in as a different user (TechExplorer). Navigated to /profile/user-5 showing NewsHound's public profile: avatar, join date ('Joined over 3 years ago'), reputation (4,521), bio paragraph, interest tags (Journalism, Politics, Economics, History), and their thread list. No 'Edit Profile' button was shown (correctly, since it's not the logged-in user's own profile).\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-11\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"No edit functionality for posts/replies\", \"actual\": \"Inspected the thread page for the user's own thread and own reply (author = logged-in user TechExplorer). No 'Edit' button, pencil icon, or any affordance to edit a published thread or reply exists in the DOM (searched all buttons/links for 'edit' text — none found besides the unrelated 'Edit Profile' button on the profile page). Since posts/replies cannot be edited at all, there is no way for an 'Edited' mark or last-edit-time to ever appear.\", \"evidence\": \"document.querySelectorAll('button,a') text search for /edit/i returned []. Article HTML for own thread shows only upvote button, category link, author link, timestamp, heading, content paragraph, reply/view counts — no edit control.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-12\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"No rich text editing/rendering support\", \"actual\": \"The thread content editor is a plain textarea with no formatting toolbar (no bold/link/list buttons). Entered markdown-style content '**bold**', '[link](https://example.com)', and a bullet list. After posting, the thread body rendered the raw text literally ('This is a **bold** statement, here is a [link](https://example.com), and a list: - item one - item two - item three') with no bold styling, no clickable hyperlink, and no list formatting.\", \"evidence\": \"Thread thread-1788333719812 body text rendered as plain unformatted string containing literal markdown syntax.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"FT-13\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Logged out (header reverted to Login/Register). Logged back in as tech@example.com. Profile page still showed the edited bio ('QA-updated bio: I love testing forums and building reliable software.'), interest tags (Testing, Automation, Coffee), and the 'Threads by TechExplorer (3)' list including the thread created earlier in this session. On the created thread's page, the upvote button correctly remembered the prior vote: clicking it toggled the count from 1 back to 0 (un-voting) rather than incrementing to 2, confirming the user's per-thread vote/interaction state was preserved across logout/login.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CS-14\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Clicked 'Create Thread' with empty Category, Title, and Content fields. Submission was blocked and a toast notification 'Please fill in all fields' appeared; no navigation away from /new-thread occurred.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CS-15\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"No duplicate content submission prevention\", \"actual\": \"Posted a reply with text 'Duplicate submission test reply.'. Then typed the exact same text again into the reply box and clicked 'Post Reply' as a separate action. The system accepted it without any warning, creating two identical replies from the same user in the same thread ('Replies (3)' shows the text